1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,599
Sealed types closed hierarchies inside the
language. Platforms still faced a
different leak: on the classic classpath,
every public type in every jar was fair
game. Internal packages were only a naming
convention.

2
00:00:13,719 --> 00:00:21,399
Reflection and deep dependency graphs made
"please don't touch that" unenforceable.
Accidental coupling across jars was
normal.

3
00:00:21,519 --> 00:00:31,359
So teams asked a practical question: can
we declare, up front, what a library
requires and what it actually exports?
Packages gave us neighborhoods.

4
00:00:31,479 --> 00:00:44,199
Modules give those neighborhoods a customs
checkpoint: you may be public inside your
module and still invisible outside it
unless exported. That single rule repairs
years of "but it compiled on my classpath"
accidents.

5
00:00:44,319 --> 00:00:51,799
That is the Java Platform Module System —
JPMS. Modules restore strong encapsulation
the classpath erased.

6
00:00:51,919 --> 00:01:03,759
The feature landed with Java 9 and
reshaped how the JDK itself is packaged —
which is why even classpath applications
feel module-related error messages when
they touch JDK internals.

7
00:01:03,879 --> 00:01:21,359
A module is a named collection of packages
with an explicit boundary. The declaration
lives in module-info.java. Look at this
java for a moment. Walk the meaning.
module com.shop.app names this module.

8
00:01:21,479 --> 00:01:37,559
requires com.shop.core states an explicit
dependency — the module graph is not a
pile of jars hoping their transitive types
appear. exports com.shop.app.api makes
only that package usable to other modules.

9
00:01:37,679 --> 00:01:42,279
Sibling packages in the same module can
stay hidden even if their types are
public.

10
00:01:42,399 --> 00:01:55,279
Public no longer means "visible to the
whole classpath world." opens appears when
reflective frameworks need deep access to
otherwise hidden packages. Opening is a
deliberate door, not the default.

11
00:01:55,399 --> 00:02:02,439
Look at this java for a moment. Opening
everything "just to make it work"
recreates classpath porosity with module
syntax.

12
00:02:02,559 --> 00:02:13,799
Prefer exporting a stable API and opening
only what a known tool requires, ideally
to a named module. Migration can be
incremental. Many applications still run
on the classpath.

13
00:02:13,919 --> 00:02:26,679
You can modularize libraries gradually, or
stay on the classpath while learning the
module graph of the platform itself. JPMS
is not an all-or-nothing cliff for every
codebase on day one.

14
00:02:26,799 --> 00:02:36,039
What problem do modules solve in one
sentence? Reliable configuration and
stronger encapsulation than a flat
classpath. Missing requires fail early.

15
00:02:36,159 --> 00:02:49,799
Illegal access to non-exported packages
fails clearly instead of succeeding by
accident. Modules alone do not equal good
architecture. You can still invent tangled
exports and leaky APIs inside a modular
build.

16
00:02:49,919 --> 00:03:00,279
The module system enforces boundaries you
declare; it does not invent a clean domain
for you. Picture two jars on an old
classpath: shop-core and shop-app.

17
00:03:00,399 --> 00:03:11,959
A public class named InternalHasher lived
in com.shop.core.internal. Nothing stopped
shop-app from calling it. A year later the
core team renamed the hasher and half the
apps broke.

18
00:03:12,079 --> 00:03:26,199
Packages said "internal" in the name; the
platform did not enforce it. With modules,
com.shop.core simply does not export
com.shop.core.internal. The app must use
the exported API.

19
00:03:26,319 --> 00:03:33,639
That failure happens at launch or compile
time for modular apps, not as a surprise
NoSuchMethodError after a refactor.

20
00:03:33,759 --> 00:03:45,319
If you are maintaining a library,
exporting fewer packages is usually kinder
than exporting everything "for
flexibility." Flexibility for callers
becomes fragility for you. Start narrow.

21
00:03:45,439 --> 00:03:59,679
Export more only when a real client need
appears. The same advice applies to opens:
grant reflective access specifically,
never as a blanket default. The classpath
erased strong encapsulation.

22
00:03:59,799 --> 00:04:11,439
Modules restored explicit requires,
exports, and careful opens. Incremental
migration kept classpath apps in the real
world. Encapsulation at the jar boundary
is settled enough to build with.

23
00:04:11,559 --> 00:04:22,319
Inside those boundaries, a different
everyday friction returns: arrays still
force a fixed length, while real features
— carts, timelines, search hits — grow and
shrink.

24
00:04:22,439 --> 00:04:28,519
How do we hold an ordered sequence without
hand-rolled resizing? That pressure is
what lists exist to answer.
